! The same silicon on the whole unshifted 4x4x4 grid, with no symmetry at all.
!
! The other half of the pair whose first half is si-epsilon-unshifted.in. An
! unshifted Monkhorst-Pack grid *is* closed under the point group -- every one of
! the 3072 rotation images of this one lands back on it -- so the same k-sample
! can be run two ways: reduced to 8 points with the response symmetrised (that
! file), or whole at 64 points with the symmetrisation switched off entirely
! (this one). The two must give the same dielectric tensor, and nothing is
! shared between the routes except the Sternheimer solve, so agreeing is the
! only check `symmetrize_directional` has -- `ph.x` computes the wedge route
! alone.
!
! `nosym` is what keeps the grid whole here, and it is only safe *because* the
! grid is unshifted. On a shifted grid the same combination is refused, since
! there the reduction has taken something out that nothing puts back.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 2, celldm(1) = 10.20, nat = 2, ntyp = 1,
    ecutwfc = 18.0
    nosym = .true.
    noinv = .true.
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-12
    mixing_beta = 0.7
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 Si  28.086  Si.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (alat)
 Si 0.00 0.00 0.00
 Si 0.25 0.25 0.25
K_POINTS (automatic)
 4 4 4 0 0 0
